on my exploration of the repository, I found clear examples in **[`doc/examples/BasicsFFT.hpp`](file:///psi/home/ext-nobile_a/rag-scientific-code-agent/data/raw/ippl/doc/examples/BasicsFFT.hpp)**. Here's how to set up and run a forward FFT:

## Setting Up an IPPL Field for Forward FFT

### 1. Define the Input Real Field
```cpp
// Create your real-valued input field (typically with guard cells)
ippl::FieldLayout<dim> layoutInput;  
Mesh_t meshInput(inputDomain, hx_input, origin); 
field_type_real fieldInput(meshInput, layoutInput);
```

### 2. Configure FFT Parameters and Define Output Field Layout
The output domain for real-to-complex transforms requires special sizing (one dimension is halved):

```cpp
ippl::ParameterList fftParams;
fftParams.add("use_heffte_defaults", true);          // Use heffte defaults recommended settings
fftParams.add("r2c_direction", 0);                   // Dimension to pack: kx=0, ky=1 or kz=2

// Compute half-size output dimension (N/2 + 1 unique modes for real data)
std::array<int, dim> pt = {64, 64, 64};              // Total size per dimension  
ippl::Vector<double, 3> hx     = {{pt[0]}};          // Grid spacing

if (fftParams.get<int>("r2c_direction") == 0)        // Direction 1: ky=0, kx=1 or kz=2
    ownedOutput[0] = ippl::Index(pt[0] / 2 + 1);
ownedOutput[1] = ippl::Index(pt[1]); 
ownedOutput[2] = ippl::Index(pt[2]);

ippl::FieldLayout<dim> layoutOutput(MPI_COMM_WORLD, ownedOutput, isParallel);  
Mesh_t meshOutput(ownedOutput, hx_output, origin);    
field_type_complex fieldOutput(meshOutput, layoutOutput);  // Complex-valued output
```

### 3. Create the FFT Object
```cpp
typedef ippl::FFT<ippl::RCTransform, field_type_real> FFT_type;  
std::unique_ptr<FFT_type> fft = std::make_unique<FFT_type>(layoutInput, layoutOutput, fftParams);  
// Also works for CCTransform (complex-to-complex) or CosTransform variants
```

### 4. Run a Forward Transform on the Field
```cpp
fft->transform(ippl::FORWARD, fieldInput, fieldOutput);    // FORWARD = +1/0 
fft->transform(ippl::BACKWARD, fieldInput, fieldOutput);   // BACKWARD = -1 (inverse)
```

For **in-place** transforms on complex fields: `ccfft.transform(-l,complexField2)` — note that inverse transforms use `-1` for backward direction.

